 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** O(1) per query, as it involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations and memory accesses (4 samples) [S5].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** O(1) auxiliary memory per query, assuming the grid is accessible [S5].
-*   **Performance:** Fast and efficient, making it suitable for real-time applications [S5]. Hardware acceleration is often available via texture sampling units in GPUs [S9, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally inexpensive, provides smoother results than nearest-neighbor interpolation [S5].
-*   **Limitations:** Introduces blurring (low-pass filtering effect) due to its linear nature [S5]. Discontinuous gradients at cell boundaries can cause artifacts in high-frequency details [S5][S12, unofficial]. Not suitable for reconstructing signals with high curvature or sharp edges without additional processing [S5].
-*   **When to use:** When computational efficiency is critical and high-frequency detail preservation is not the primary goal, such as in initial volume rendering passes or texture sampling [S5].
-*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity reconstruction of sharp edges or high-frequency details is required, or when C1 continuity is necessary [S5][S12, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per query, as it involves a fixed number of arithmetic operations (4 multiplications, 3 additions) and memory accesses (4 samples) [S5].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ auxiliary memory per query, assuming the grid is accessible in $O(1)$ time [S5].
+*   **Accuracy:** Provides C⁰ continuity. The approximation error is of order $O(h^2)$, where $h$ is the grid spacing [S5]. It is suitable for smooth functions but less accurate for those with higher curvature.
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally efficient, simple to implement, and provides smoother results than nearest-neighbor interpolation [S5].
+*   **Limitations:** Discontinuous gradients at cell boundaries can cause artifacts in high-frequency details or when used for gradient-based optimization (e.g., in differentiable rendering) [S5, S15]. It is not suitable for non-uniformly sampled data without significant modification [S12, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When performance is critical and the data is relatively smooth, or as a component in higher-order methods (e.g., trilinear) [S5].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-frequency detail preservation is required (use higher-order interpolation or edge-aware methods) or when the data is highly non-uniform [S12, S17, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Bilinear interpolation is a fundamental technique in computer graphics and image processing, extending linear interpolation to two dimensions [S5]. It has been widely used for tasks such as image resizing, texture mapping, and sampling in volume rendering [S5]. The method is based on the tensor product of linear interpolation kernels [S5]. It is commonly implemented in hardware via texture units in GPUs [S9, unofficial].
+Bilinear interpolation is a fundamental technique in computer graphics and image processing. It was widely adopted in the early days of digital visualization for texture mapping and volume rendering. The method is described in technical references for fire dynamics simulation visualization tools like Smokeview [S2, unofficial] and in general computer graphics literature [S5]. It is also used in Fourier space reconstruction for tomography, where it retrieves values from polar grids to Cartesian grids [S11, unofficial]. In modern differentiable rendering, it is implemented via hardware-accelerated operations like `grid_sample` in PyTorch [S4, S10, unofficial] and is a core component of spatially varying functions in Gaussian Splatting variants like SVGS [S15].
 
